; Host - which target this compiler binary itself runs on.
;
; The self-hosted compiler cannot detect its host at runtime: it has no
; `uname`, and a freestanding binary has nothing else to ask. So the
; answer is baked in at the moment the compiler is COMPILED, by the
; same per-target module selection the standard library's syscall
; tables use (`Foo.{os}-{arch}.ax` before `Foo.{os}.ax` before
; `Foo.ax`). Compiling the compiler for linux-x86_64 selects the
; linux-x86_64 file here, and the resulting binary defaults to emitting
; linux-x86_64 code.
;
; That inheritance is what makes the bootstrap self-describing. stage0
; defaults `--target` to `Target::host`, so on a Linux runner stage0
; builds a stage1 whose `hostTarget` is linux-x86_64, which builds a
; linux stage2, and so on down the ladder - with no target argument
; passed anywhere.
;
; Before this module the default was the literal string
; "darwin-aarch64", written into `main.ax`. `check-bootstrap.sh` passes
; no target argument and runs on `ubuntu-latest`, so the Linux
; fixpoint job was compiling the compiler with a Mach-O triple: `llc`
; produced a Mach-O object and `cc` could not link it. The fixpoint was
; only ever reached on macOS ARM.
;
; There is deliberately NO fallback `Host.ax`. A target with no file
; here fails at compile time with `cannot read module: Host`, which is
; the loud failure; a fallback would silently hand the new target
; somebody else's triple.
(pub :: hostTarget String)

(pub fn (hostTarget) "darwin-aarch64")
